 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Gate drive",
   "page": 2,
   "text": "Threshold lies between -1 V and -3 V at -250 µA with gate and drain joined. RDS(ON) is guaranteed at two gate levels: 28 mΩ max at -10 V (ID = -5 A) and 35 mΩ max at -4.5 V (ID = -4 A). Typical curves put it near 20 mΩ at -10 V and 25 mΩ at -4.5 V over 5 to 30 A (Figure 3), and show it rising steeply once the gate falls below about 3.5 V (Figure 4). The gate takes ±20 V at most with leakage up to ±100 nA. Charging the gate to -10 V takes 53.1 nC typical (26.5 nC to -4.5 V), with 7.1 nC gate-source and 12.6 nC gate-drain; the internal gate resistance is 8 Ω typical."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Temperature",
   "page": 3,
   "text": "On-resistance grows with junction temperature: normalised to 1 at about 25 °C, it reads about 0.63 at -50 °C and 1.75 at 150 °C at VGS = -4.5 V, ID = -5 A (Figure 6). Threshold magnitude drops as ambient rises, from about 2.3 V at -50 °C to about 1.15 V at 150 °C at -250 µA (Figure 8). In the transfer curves the hotter part conducts at a lower gate voltage throughout the range shown (Figure 2)."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Heat and current",
   "page": 2,
   "text": "Every rating marked Note 5 holds for a part on FR-4 with 2 oz copper and a one-inch-square pad. On that board junction-to-ambient resistance is 60 °C/W and the part dissipates 2 W at 25 °C ambient (1.3 W at 70 °C), with -7 A continuous drain current (-5.6 A at 70 °C). Held at a 25 °C case, junction-to-case resistance is 7.2 °C/W, dissipation 17.3 W and current -18.2 A (-14.5 A at 70 °C case). The transient thermal curves of Figure 13 use 101 °C/W as their junction-to-ambient base."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Pulsed current, avalanche and safe operating area",
   "page": 4,
   "text": "Drain current may pulse to -50 A for 10 µs at 1 % duty. The part is rated for -35.5 A and 62.9 mJ of avalanche into 0.1 mH. Figure 12 gives single-pulse limits at TA = +25 °C with a 150 °C junction maximum: an RDS(on) boundary rising to about 50 A, then lines for 100 µs, 1 ms, 10 ms, 100 ms, 1 s, 10 s and DC, all ending at 60 V."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Body diode",
   "page": 2,
   "text": "The built-in diode carries up to -2 A continuously, drops -0.7 V typical and -1.2 V at most at -1 A, and recovers in 20 ns typical with 14 nC of charge from -5 A at 100 A/µs. Figure 9 shows its forward drop falling as temperature rises."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Switching",
   "page": 2,
   "text": "With VGS = -10 V, VDS = -30 V, a 3 Ω gate resistor and ID = -5 A, typical turn-on delay is 6 ns and rise 7.1 ns; turn-off delay is 110 ns and fall 62 ns."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Construction and compliance",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "SOT223 case of green molding compound rated UL 94V-0, moisture sensitivity level 1 to J-STD-020. Leads are annealed matte tin over a copper frame, solderable to MIL-STD-202 Method 208. The part weighs about 0.112 g. It is lead-free and RoHS compliant (2002/95/EC and 2011/65/EU), and halogen- and antimony-free: under 900 ppm bromine, under 900 ppm chlorine, under 1500 ppm of the two together and under 1000 ppm antimony compounds. It is qualified to AEC-Q101."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Marking",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "The top face carries the Diodes logo, the code P6023 and a date code YWW: Y is the year code (4 = 2014), written with or without a bar over it, and WW is the week, 01 to 53."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Package outline",
   "page": 6,
   "text": "The leads are measured at a gauge plane 0.25 mm above the seating plane, and leave the body at 0° to 10° to that plane. The body's side faces have a 7° draft top and bottom."
  }
